Kingsway Cuts is a fictional barbershop on Bloor West in Toronto, with three barbers and sample data. You can book a cut, then see it from the owner's side.

## Problem

A busy barbershop loses customers in two ways. Some call while every barber is busy with a cut, and nobody can answer. Others walk past because they can't tell how long the wait is. A paper appointment book and a phone that rings in the middle of a cut make both worse. And every no-show costs a full slot.

## What it does

- **Choose your barber.** Customers pick a service (classic haircut, skin fade, haircut and beard, hot towel shave, kids cut) and then a barber. Or they choose "First available" to see every open time across the shop.
- **Walk-in wait times.** A board on the site shows whether the shop is open and how long the wait is right now. The owner updates it from their phone in two taps.
- **A deposit holds the chair.** A $10 deposit by card (through Stripe) secures each online booking. The time is held while the customer pays. The deposit is refunded if they cancel at least 24 hours ahead.
- **Move or cancel without calling.** The confirmation has a link where customers can change their time themselves. A cancelled slot goes back on the calendar instead of sitting empty.
- **Automatic reminders.** Customers get a confirmation right away and a reminder 24 hours before the appointment. Both come by email, and by text for customers who tick "Text me". Replying STOP turns texts off, and no reminder text goes out at night.
- **An owner calendar.** See the day by barber, the next customer, today's bookings and booked value, and every message each customer was sent.
- **Edit it yourself.** Change prices, opening hours, barbers' hours and breaks, and the home page text in the admin area. No developer needed.
- **A "Book a chair" button anywhere.** One line of code adds the same booking to another website, such as an existing WordPress or Squarespace site.

## How it helps your business

- **Customers book at 11 p.m.** instead of calling during your busiest hour.
- **No double bookings.** If two people try to take the last slot at the same moment, only one gets it. The other simply sees fresh times.
- **Fewer no-shows.** A deposit means people turn up, reminders go out on their own, and a cancelled appointment frees the chair for someone else.
- **Walk-ins come in when it's quiet,** because they can see the wait before they leave home.

## How it's built

- **Times are worked out on the server** from each barber's hours, breaks and the length of each service, with daylight saving time handled properly.
- **A dark, editorial design** with real photos, tuned so text over the photos still meets accessibility contrast.
- **Automated tests** cover the availability rules, two customers racing for the same slot, and reminders.
- **Cheap to run.** Next.js on Cloudflare Workers with a D1 database, and a scheduled job that sends due reminders every five minutes.

## Stack

Next.js (App Router), TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Cloudflare Workers, Cloudflare D1 with Drizzle ORM, Stripe Checkout, Twilio, Zod and Vitest.

## Outcome

A working barbershop site you can try end to end: book a skin fade with the first available barber and pay the deposit with Stripe's test card (4242 4242 4242 4242, no real money moves), move it from the confirmation link, then log in as the owner and see the booking, the customer and every message on the calendar. The sample data resets every hour.
